\DOC BIGNUM_LDIGITIZE_TAC

\TYPE {BIGNUM_LDIGITIZE_TAC : string -> term -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Given a string prefix and a term denoting a multi-word memory read of a bignum,
it splits that bignum into individual 64-bit digit variables, replacing
occurrences of the whole-bignum read in the assumptions with a
{bignum_of_wordlist} of fresh digit variables and adding one assumption per
digit.

\DESCRIBE
{s : string} is the name prefix from which the fresh digit variables are
generated, by appending the index numbers {0 ... n-1} to it. {tm : term} is the
term designating the bignum read from memory — a read of a byte range of a
given word length, in one observed trace written via {bignum_from_memory} —
that is to be digitized. Writing {t} for this read term and {n} for the word
length appearing in it, every assumption containing {t} is rewritten with {t}
replaced by {bignum_of_wordlist [x1; ...; xn]} over the fresh variables
{x1 ... xn}, and {n} new assumptions are appended, each equating a single-word
read at the corresponding byte offset from the base address with the matching
digit variable:
{
   A ?- u
   ================================================  BIGNUM_LDIGITIZE_TAC s t
   A[bignum_of_wordlist [x1;...;xn]/t], <n digit-read equations> ?- u
}
\noindent Several applications in sequence accumulate digit assumptions for
different bignums.

\FAILURE
Fails when {tm} is not of the expected shape of a memory read of a byte range
with an explicit numeral word count; observed failures are for {tm} a read of a
single 64-bit word, a bare state variable, or a {bignum_of_wordlist}
application. These failures arise inside the tactic's own decomposition of {tm}
(destructing its applications and reading off a numeral length), not from
anything supplied as a tactic.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this transformation, always on goals whose
conclusion is an {eventually} machine-step goal and whose assumptions mention
the read term.

\ENDDOC
